<h3>Layer1 touch screen</h3>

<p>
    Layer1 is a full-screen touch interface for a 3.5" Raspberry Pi screen. This plugin
    serves it at <a href="plugin/layer1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/plugin/layer1/</a>,
    so you can try it in this browser before the screen is set up.
</p>
<p>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="plugin/layer1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i class="fas fa-external-link-alt"></i> Open Layer1</a>
    <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/tushgaurav/layer1/blob/main/docs/setup.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><i class="fas fa-book"></i> Setup Guide</a>
</p>

<h4>Status</h4>
<div class="form-horizontal">
    <div class="control-group">
        <label class="control-label">Kiosk service</label>
        <div class="controls" style="padding-top: 5px">
            <span class="label" data-bind="css: kioskCss, text: kioskText"></span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="control-group">
        <label class="control-label">Screen</label>
        <div class="controls" style="padding-top: 5px">
            <span class="label" data-bind="css: pairCss, text: pairText"></span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="control-group">
        <div class="controls">
            <button class="btn btn-small" data-bind="click: refresh, enable: !checking()"><i class="fas fa-sync-alt" data-bind="css: {'fa-spin': checking}"></i> Check Again</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h4>Put it on the screen</h4>
<p>
    Run these on the Pi over SSH (<code>ssh pi@octopi.local</code>). Layer1 never changes your
    system by itself; only these scripts do, when you run them.
</p>
<ol>
    <li>
        Get the setup scripts:
        <pre>git clone https://github.com/tushgaurav/layer1 ~/layer1</pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        Turn on the screen driver, then reboot. The built-in <code>piscreen</code> driver works for most
        ILI9486 screens (Waveshare (A) and its clones). For others, see
        <a href="https://github.com/tushgaurav/layer1/blob/main/docs/screen-setup.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">screen setup</a>.
        <pre>sudo bash ~/layer1/pi-setup/setup-screen.sh piscreen
sudo reboot</pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        Install the kiosk. It adds a minimal X server and Chromium (no desktop) and a service that
        opens Layer1 on the screen at boot:
        <pre>sudo bash ~/layer1/pi-setup/install-kiosk.sh</pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        Pair the screen. It shows <strong>Pair this screen with OctoPrint</strong>. Click
        <strong>Allow</strong> on the access request from Layer1 that appears here in OctoPrint.
    </li>
</ol>

<h4>Settings</h4>
<p>
    Temperature presets, limits, filament lengths and webcam options are in
    <code>{{ plugin_layer1_config_path }}</code>, which is created the first time Layer1 opens.
    The <a href="https://github.com/tushgaurav/layer1/blob/main/docs/setup.md#settings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">setup guide</a>
    lists every key.
</p>
